Complex Regional Pain Syndrome
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Disproportionate regional pain, Severe mechanical allodynia, Vasomotor/sudomotor asymmetry

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-009
Reason for Visit: Excruciating, unmanageable distal limb pain and swelling with severe skin hypersensitivity.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden, excruciating burning pain in the right lower leg with dramatic color changes and an inability to tolerate clothing touching the skin. Immediate clinical assessment is required to exclude an acute deep vein thrombosis, compartment syndrome, or acute limb ischemia before regular in-hours consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My right foot is on fire and swollen. Even the bed sheet resting on it makes me scream in agony."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports continuous, intense regional pain in the right distal lower extremity following a minor ankle sprain six weeks ago. The pain is completely disproportionate in time and degree to the usual course of a sprain. Over the last 24 hours, the patient has developed severe mechanical allodynia, causing extreme pain from normally innocuous stimuli such as clothing or air currents. There is pronounced temperature asymmetry, with the affected limb feeling noticeably warmer than the left. The patient notes distinct skin color changes, presenting today as an asymmetrical deep red, mottled appearance. Trophic changes are beginning to appear, with thin, shiny, glossy skin over the dorsum of the foot.
Functional Status: Bedbound; completely unable to weight-bear or tolerate footwear.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to mobilize to the bathroom due to extreme allodynia.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Highly anxious, crying in pain, fiercely guarding the right lower extremity.
Physical Exam: Right lower extremity reveals distinct vasomotor asymmetry, with the right foot deep red, mottled, and visibly edematous compared to the left. Skin temperature is palpably warmer on the right. Exquisite mechanical allodynia is present; light touch with a cotton wisp elicits severe pain withdrawal. Sudomotor asymmetry is noted with localized hyperhidrosis on the right sole. Peripheral pulses are bounding bilaterally; capillary refill is brisk. No calf tenderness or Homan's sign. Motor exam is limited by extreme pain, but gross toe movement is intact.
Environmental Observations: Foot of the bed completely stripped of sheets; heavy reliance on crutches lying on the floor.
Medication Reconciliation: Ibuprofen and Paracetamol providing zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS) Type 1 - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Flare)
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of isolated calf swelling, no deep venous tenderness, and presence of extreme cutaneous allodynia and hyperhidrosis.

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, absence of a distinct portal of entry, and the presence of severe mechanical allodynia out of proportion to the erythema.

Acute Compartment Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal pulses, brisk capillary refill, and pain triggered by light touch rather than passive stretch of the muscle compartment.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, agonizing flare of severe neuropathic and autonomic dysfunction in a distal extremity. The severity of the allodynia and vasomotor changes triggered an after-hours call due to fears of an acute vascular or infectious catastrophe. Delaying assessment would result in unrelenting, excruciating pain and severe psychological distress. Urgent after-hours physical examination was clinically mandatory to definitively differentiate this profound neuropathic phenomenon from limb-threatening emergencies like DVT or compartment syndrome. Formulating the clinical diagnosis of CRPS on-site allowed for the immediate initiation of targeted, membrane-stabilizing neuropathic analgesics, thereby fulfilling MBS criteria for urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Pregabalin 75mg orally STAT, followed by 75mg at night. Prescribed a short course of Tapentadol 50mg PRN for severe breakthrough pain.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By engaging in frequent, very gentle self-massage of the limb using a soft cloth, the patient provides continuous sensory desensitization, progressively reducing the extreme hypersensitivity over time.
Step 2: By persistently attempting partial weight-bearing with crutches despite discomfort, the patient maintains vital joint mobility, preventing rapid muscle atrophy and permanent contractures.
Step 3: By taking the Pregabalin at night consistently, the patient directly stabilizes the hyperactive nerves, guaranteeing periods of restorative sleep free from burning pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to a specialized Chronic Pain Management Clinic and specialized physiotherapy.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provision of a bed cradle to keep sheets off the affected limb.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 3 days to titrate neuropathic medications.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the Emergency Department immediately if the foot becomes cold, turns pale or blue, or if you lose the ability to move your toes completely.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; early recognition and aggressive multidisciplinary treatment are required for the best prognosis.
Clinical Course: The condition is notoriously difficult to treat. The prescribed medications will start to calm the nerve firing over the next few days, but complete resolution requires sustained, dedicated physical therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for urgent medical review if you develop a high fever or if a spreading, well-demarcated area of red, hot skin develops, indicating a superimposed infection.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your nervous system has drastically overreacted to the initial sprain. It is currently misinterpreting normal touch as severe pain and mismanaging blood flow. Standard painkillers do not work. The new medications specifically target and calm these misfiring nerves.
Actionable Steps: Start the neuropathic pain medication tonight. Gently touch and move the foot frequently, even when it hurts, to retrain the brain's pain pathways.

